    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "This thesis analyzes the effort effects of census cycle redistricting on United States House of Representative members. We compile data on the 107th, 112th, and 117th Congresses, as the sessions subjected to redistricting cycles, focusing on roll call attendance. Using census information, state district maps, and MIT House election data, we determined which congresspeople were subject to redistricting over the course of their term. Redistricting is treated as a quasi-random experiment, with the announcement of the new state district map as the treatment. This paper evaluates whether redistricting acts as a perceived term limit for incumbents, if representatives are forced into a district where another party holds a majority, or by adding another competitive incumbent to their district. Therefore, without the introduction of explicit term limits to Congress, we evaluate the effects of a term limit through the redistricting process as measured in roll call votes. We perform a difference in difference analysis on redistricted congresspeople with like representatives whose district was unchanged. This links congressional effort allocation measured in roll call votes to their reelection incentives if redistricting is viewed as a term limit. In our empirical analysis, we find little or no impact on roll call attendance from the announcement of new district maps, dependent on the congressional session.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
